 A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals 2.1 Field properties for even q q 2.2 Coordinates of the orthogonal BM unitals 2.3 The automorphism group of the orthogonal BM unital, even q q 2.4 The equivalence of orthogonal BM unitals, even q q 2.5 Fano planes in Buekenhout-Metz unitals 2.6 Contribution of this paper 3 Main results for BM-unitals, even q q 3.1 Existence of Fano planes in even order orthogonal BM unitals 3.2 Number of BM-special Fano planes 4 Tits Unitals 4.1 Discussion 4.2 Examples 5 Conclusion References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2403.05013v1 [math.CO] 08 Mar 2024 A Note on Fano Planes in Orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz Unitals of Even Order Wen-Ai Jackson Note: School of Mathematical Sciences, University of Adelaide, Adelaide 5005, Australia, Email: wen.jackson@adelaide.edu.au Peter Wild Note: Royal Holloway, University of London, Egham TW20 0EX, Email: peterrwild@gmail.com Abstract An O’Nan configuration in a unital is a set of four lines forming a quadrilateral, where the six intersections of pairs of lines are points of the unital. In 2019 Feng and Li [ 6 ] elegantly construct O’Nan configurations in orthogonal and Tits Buekenhout-Metz unitals. We extend their work by extending their construction to a Fano plane embedded in the orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unital of even order. We deduce that there exist O’Nan configurations in orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unitals different to those of Feng and Li, and make a conjecture about Fano planes embedded in orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unitals. AMS code: 51E20 Keywords: unital, Buekenhout-Metz unital, O’Nan configuration 1 Introduction A unital of order n n is a 2– ( n 3 + 1 , n + 1 , 1 ) (n^{3}+1,n+1,1) design. Unitals of prime power order q q exist in the Desarguesian projective planes PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , as the absolute points and lines of a hermitian polarity. Such unitals are called classical unitals. Buekenhout [ 4 ] constructed unitals in 2–dimensional translation planes. This was generalised by Metz [ 8 ] to construct non-classical unitals in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) . These unitals are called Buekenhout-Metz (BM) unitals. They have the property that every line of PG ​ ( 2 , q ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q) meets the unital in 1 or q + 1 q+1 points. Further, the unital has exactly one point on the line at infinity, called the special point. For a comprehensive treatise on unitals, see Barwick and Ebert [ 2 ] . An O’Nan configuration in a unital is a set of four distinct lines forming a quadrilateral, whose six points of intersection lie on the unital. In 1972, O’Nan [ 9 ] noted that these configurations do not occur in the classical unital. It was conjectured by Piper [ 10 ] that this property characterizes the classical unital within the class of all unitals. In 2019, Feng and Li [ 6 ] showed the existence of O’Nan configurations in orthogonal BM unitals in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , for both q q even and odd. They also show the existence of O’Nan configurations in the Buekenhout-Tits unital. In this paper, we use their results to show the existence of Fano planes in BM unitals of even order, which supports the following conjecture: Conjecture 1 . Suppose q q is even. Every non-classical unital 𝒰 {\mathcal{U}} in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) contains a Fano plane if q ≥ 4 q\geq 4 . 2 Buekenhout-Metz unitals 2.1 Field properties for even q q We give properties of 𝔽 q 2 \mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} where q q is an even prime power. In general, consider a prime p p and a positive integer m m with q = p m > 2 q=p^{m}>2 . Suppose that d d is any divisor of m m , then the trace function from 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} to 𝔽 p d \mathbb{F}_{p^{d}} is Tr p m / p d ​ ( x ) = x + x p d + x p d + 1 + ⋯ + x p m − d . \mbox{Tr}_{p^{m}/p^{d}}(x)=x+x^{p^{d}}+x^{p^{d+1}}+\cdots+x^{p^{m-d}}. Suppose now that q = 2 m > 2 q=2^{m}>2 . The absolute trace of an element x x of 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} is Tr 2 m / 2 ​ ( x ) \mbox{Tr}_{2^{m}/2}(x) . Let 𝒯 0 , 𝒯 1 \mathcal{T}_{0},\mathcal{T}_{1} be the set of elements of 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} with absolute trace 0 and 1 respectively. From [ 2 , Lemma 4.21] , we can choose δ ∈ 𝔽 q 2 \ 𝔽 q \delta\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\backslash\mathbb{F}_{q} such that δ q = δ + 1 \delta^{q}=\delta+1 , δ 2 = δ + v \delta^{2}=\delta+v for some v ∈ 𝒯 1 , v ≠ 1 v\in\mathcal{T}_{1},v\neq 1 . Then { 1 , δ } \{1,\delta\} is a basis for 𝔽 q 2 \mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} over 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} . For any element x x of 𝔽 q 2 \mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} we will also use the notation x = x + x q = Tr q 2 / q ​ ( x ) . \boxed{x}=x+x^{q}=\mbox{Tr}_{q^{2}/q}(x). For k = k 0 + k 1 ​ δ k=k_{0}+k_{1}\delta , with k 0 , k 1 ∈ 𝔽 q k_{0},k_{1}\in\mathbb{F}_{q} it is easy to calculate k q = ( k 0 + k 1 ) + k 1 ​ δ = k + k 1 k^{q}=(k_{0}+k_{1})+k_{1}\delta=k+k_{1} , k q + 1 = k q ​ k = k 0 2 + k 0 ​ k 1 + v ​ k 1 2 k^{q+1}=k^{q}k=k_{0}^{2}+k_{0}k_{1}+vk_{1}^{2} , k = k + k q = k 1 \boxed{k}=k+k^{q}=k_{1} , k ​ δ = ( k ​ δ ) + ( k ​ δ ) q = k 0 + k 1 \boxed{k\delta}=(k\delta)+(k\delta)^{q}=k_{0}+k_{1} and k ​ δ q = ( k ​ δ q ) + ( k q ​ δ ) = k 0 \boxed{k\delta^{q}}=(k\delta^{q})+(k^{q}\delta)=k_{0} . 2.2 Coordinates of the orthogonal BM unitals Ebert [ 5 ] showed that an orthogonal Buekenhout-Metz unital 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) is projectively equivalent to the following set of points 𝒰 a , b = { ( x , a x 2 + b x q + 1 + r , 1 ) | r ∈ 𝔽 q , x ∈ 𝔽 q 2 } ∪ { T ∞ = ( 0 , 1 , 0 ) } . {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}=\{(x,ax^{2}+bx^{q+1}+r,1)\bigm|r\in\mathbb{F}_{q},\ x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\}\cup\{T_{\infty}=(0,1,0)\}. where the discriminant d = a q + 1 ( b q + b ) 2 d=\frac{a^{q+1}}{(b^{q}+b)^{2}} belongs to 𝒯 0 \mathcal{T}_{0} , the set of elements of absolute trace 0 of 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} . Further 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} is classical if and only if a = 0 a=0 . Consequently, an affine point P = ( x , y , 1 ) P=(x,y,1) belongs to 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} if and only if a ​ x 2 + a q ​ x 2 ​ q + ( b + b q ) ​ x q + 1 + y + y q = 0 . ax^{2}+a^{q}x^{2q}+(b+b^{q})x^{q+1}+y+y^{q}=0. (1) 2.3 The automorphism group of the orthogonal BM unital, even q q This was investigated by Ebert [ 5 ] but we use the notation from [ 2 , Theorem 4.23] . We assume that the BM unital 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} is not classical, that is, a ≠ 0 a\neq 0 . Let F be the smallest subfield of 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} containing the discriminant d d . Let G G be the subgroup of the automorphism group P ​ Γ ​ L ​ ( 3 , q 2 ) \textup{P}\Gamma{\textup{L}}(3,q^{2}) of PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) leaving 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} invariant. Then G G also fixes T ∞ T_{\infty} . The order of G G is m ​ q 3 ​ ( q − 1 ) mq^{3}(q-1) where m m is the dimension of 𝔽 q 2 \mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} over F . G G has four point orbits, namely, { T ∞ } \{T_{\infty}\} , 𝒰 a , b \ T ∞ {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\backslash T_{\infty} , ℓ ∞ \ T ∞ \ell_{\infty}\backslash T_{\infty} and PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) \ ( 𝒰 a , b ∪ ℓ ∞ ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\backslash({\mathcal{U}}_{a,b}\cup\ell_{\infty}) . 2.4 The equivalence of orthogonal BM unitals, even q q From Ebert [ 5 ] , two BM unitals 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} are equivalent if and only if we can find v ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ v\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} , γ ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ \gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} and u ∈ 𝔽 q u\in\mathbb{F}_{q} and τ \tau a field automorphism of 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} with ( a ′ , b ′ ) = ( a τ γ 2 v , b τ γ q + 1 v + u ) . (a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}v,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}v+u). For q = 2 n q=2^{n} , the number of inequivalent orthogonal BM unitals in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , for n = 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 n=2,3,4,5,6 is 2 , 2 , 4 , 4 , 8 2,2,4,4,8 respectively, one of which is the classical unital [ 2 , Theorem 4.14] . Lemma 2 . For even q q , if 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} is any non-classical orthogonal BM unital, then without loss of generality we may assume 1. a = 1 a=1 , or 2. a ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} and b = δ b=\delta . Proof. From above, given 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , then 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} with ( a ′ , b ′ ) = ( a τ γ 2 ν , b τ γ q + 1 + u ) (a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(a^{\tau}\gamma^{2}\nu,\ \ b^{\tau}\gamma^{q+1}+u) is an equivalent unital if ν ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ \nu\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} , γ ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ \gamma\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} and u ∈ 𝔽 q u\in\mathbb{F}_{q} . For Part 1, we take τ = \tau= identity, γ = 1 / a \gamma=1/\sqrt{a} , ν = 1 \nu=1 and u = 0 u=0 to get ( 1 , b / ( a ) q + 1 ) (1,b/(\sqrt{a})^{q+1}) , as required. For Part 2, the proof is in Feng and Li [ 6 , Section 3.2] . ∎ 2.5 Fano planes in Buekenhout-Metz unitals It is well known that no O’Nan configuration contains the special (infinite) point T ∞ = ( 0 , 1 , 0 ) T_{\infty}=(0,1,0) of a Buekenhout-Metz unital [ 2 , Lemma 7.42] . Hence it follows there is no Fano plane containing the special point T ∞ T_{\infty} . However, it is possible that a line of the Fano plane, regarded as a line of PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , contains T ∞ T_{\infty} . Definition 3 . In PG ​ ( 2 , q ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q) , q q even, consider a BM unital 𝒰 {\mathcal{U}} with special point T ∞ T_{\infty} at infinity. We say a Fano plane in a BM unital embedded in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) with special point T ∞ T_{\infty} is a BM-special Fano plane if one of the lines of the Fano plane, when considered as a line as of PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , contains the special point T ∞ T_{\infty} ; Otherwise, we call it a BM-ordinary Fano plane . 2.6 Contribution of this paper Feng and Li [ 6 ] have three beautiful constructions of O’Nan configurations. In this paper we consider their O’Nan configurations in the orthogonal case for even q q . Their O’Nan configurations have a line which contains the special point T ∞ T_{\infty} . We begin by showing that the O’Nan configuration of Feng and Li can be extended to a Fano plane. Theorem 4 . The O’Nan configuration of Feng and Li [ 6 ] for orthogonal BM unitals of even order, can be extended to a Fano plane contained within the BM unital, that is, to a BM-special Fano plane. We further investigate the BM-special Fano planes, and prove the following: Theorem 5 . Let q = 2 m > 2 q=2^{m}>2 . Let 𝒰 1 ​ ( 1 , b ) {\mathcal{U}}_{1}(1,b) and 𝒰 2 ​ ( 1 , b ′ ) {\mathcal{U}}_{2}(1,b^{\prime}) be any two non-classical orthogonal BM unitals of PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) . Then the number of BM-special Fano planes in 𝒰 1 {\mathcal{U}}_{1} and 𝒰 2 {\mathcal{U}}_{2} are the same. Note that for q = 4 , 8 q=4,8 there is only one equivalence class of non-classical unitals, so the above theorem holds trivially. Since a Fano plane contains seven O’Nan configurations we have the following Corollary. Corollary 6 . Let q = 2 m > 2 q=2^{m}>2 . In orthogonal BM unitals of PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) , there exist O’Nan configurations, where none of the four lines of the configuration is incident with the special point T ∞ T_{\infty} of the BM unital. 3 Main results for BM-unitals, even q q 3.1 Existence of Fano planes in even order orthogonal BM unitals We now complete the proof of Theorem 4 . Feng and Li choose a ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ a\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} and β = δ \beta=\delta (see Lemma 2 (2)), and define ϕ : PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) → PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) , ϕ : ( x , y , z ) ↦ ( x q , y q , z q ) \phi\colon{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2})\rightarrow{\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}),\quad\phi\colon(x,y,z)\mapsto(x^{q},y^{q},z^{q}) which stabilises 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , fixing the line B ∞ = { ( 0 , r , 1 ) : r ∈ 𝔽 q } ∪ { ( 0 , 1 , 0 ) } B_{\infty}=\{(0,r,1)\colon r\in\mathbb{F}_{q}\}\cup\{(0,1,0)\} of 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , and no other point of 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} . They show there exists points Q , R ∈ 𝒰 a , b Q,R\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} with Q , R ∈ [ δ , 1 , 0 ] Q,R\in[\delta,1,0] , and that if P = ( 0 , 0 , 1 ) P=(0,0,1) , then the lines Q ​ R ϕ , R ​ Q ϕ , Q ​ Q ϕ , R ​ R ϕ QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi} are four lines with the six points of intersection P , Q , R , Q ϕ , R ϕ P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi} and M = Q ​ Q ϕ ∩ R ​ R ϕ ∈ B ∞ M=QQ^{\phi}\cap RR^{\phi}\in B_{\infty} lying on 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , hence forming an O’Nan configuration. We now show that this completes to a Fano plane. Let N = Q ​ R ∩ Q ϕ ​ R ϕ , N=QR\cap Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}, so N ϕ = Q ϕ ​ R ϕ ∩ Q ​ R = N N^{\phi}=Q^{\phi}R^{\phi}\cap QR=N , and N N is a fixed point. As q q is even, note that P , M , N P,M,N are collinear, and as N N is on P ​ M PM and N N is fixed, and as B ∞ B_{\infty} contains the fixed points on [ 1 , 0 , 0 ] [1,0,0] it follows that N = ( 0 , s , 1 ) N=(0,s,1) for some s ∈ 𝔽 q s\in\mathbb{F}_{q} . So N ∈ 𝒰 a , b N\in{\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} . Hence, the six lines Q ​ R ϕ , R ​ Q ϕ , Q ​ Q ϕ , R ​ R ϕ , Q ​ R , Q ϕ ​ R ϕ QR^{\phi},RQ^{\phi},QQ^{\phi},RR^{\phi},QR,Q^{\phi}R^{\phi} and the seven points P , Q , R , Q ϕ , R ϕ , M , N P,Q,R,Q^{\phi},R^{\phi},M,N all belong to 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} , and so form a Fano plane, completing the proof of Theorem 4 . 3.2 Number of BM-special Fano planes We will show that the number of BM-special Fano planes in orthogonal BM-unitals of even order depends only the order of the unital. We now calculate the general form of a BM-special Fano plane. By Section 2.3 , we may assume that the point V = ( 0 , 0 , 1 ) V=(0,0,1) belongs to the Fano plane. As the Fano plane is BM-special, there are a further two points on V ​ T ∞ VT_{\infty} , without loss of generality let these be X = ( 0 , s , 1 ) X=(0,s,1) and Y = ( 0 , t , 1 ) Y=(0,t,1) for s , t ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} , s ≠ t s\neq t . Through each point of the Fano plane lies three lines, so let ℓ x = [ 1 , x , 0 ] \ell_{x}=[1,x,0] be another line through V V , so x ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} , and let the two points of the Fano plane on ℓ x \ell_{x} be P = ( x ​ j , j , 1 ) P=(xj,j,1) and Q = ( x ​ k , k , 1 ) Q=(xk,k,1) for some j , k ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ j,k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} with j ≠ k j\neq k . So ℓ x = P ​ Q ​ V \ell_{x}=PQV . The remaining two points M , N M,N of the Fano plane are determine by M = P ​ X ∩ Q ​ Y M=PX\cap QY and N = P ​ Y ∩ Q ​ X N=PY\cap QX , and M , N , V M,N,V are collinear. Instead of writing j j , we can define h ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} , h ≠ 1 h\neq 1 by j = k ​ h j=kh . Then every BM-special Fano plane can be described by the parameters ( a , b , x , k , h , s , t ) (a,b,x,k,h,s,t) . However this description is not unique, as we may swap X X and Y Y , P P and Q Q and the line V ​ P ​ Q VPQ with the line V ​ M ​ N VMN . In any case, the number of such descriptions will be same for each Fano plane. Proof. (of Theorem 5 ) Suppose that a = a ′ = 1 a=a^{\prime}=1 and put b = b 0 + b 1 ​ e b=b_{0}+b_{1}e and b ′ = b 0 ′ + b 1 ′ ​ e b^{\prime}=b_{0}^{\prime}+b_{1}^{\prime}e . Suppose there exists a BM-special Fano plane in 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} with parameters ( 1 , b , x , k , h , s , t ) (1,b,x,k,h,s,t) . Then we will show that there exists a BM-special Fano plane in 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} with parameters ( 1 , b ′ , x ′ , k ′ , h , s , t ) (1,b^{\prime},x^{\prime},k^{\prime},h,s,t) with x ​ k = x ′ ​ k ′ xk=x^{\prime}k^{\prime} . Write θ = x ​ k \theta=xk . We have, with h = j / k ∈ 𝔽 q 2 h=j/k\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} , P = ( θ ​ h , k ​ h , 1 ) P=(\theta h,kh,1) and Q = ( θ , k , 1 ) Q=(\theta,k,1) with θ , k , h ∈ 𝔽 q 2 ∗ \theta,k,h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}^{*} , X = ( 0 , s , 1 ) X=(0,s,1) and Y = ( 0 , t , 1 ) Y=(0,t,1) with s , t ∈ 𝔽 q ∗ s,t\in\mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} . Let V = ( 0 , 0 , 1 ) V=(0,0,1) . Let M = P ​ X ∩ Q ​ Y M=PX\cap QY and N = P ​ Y ∩ Q ​ X N=PY\cap QX , so M = ( x ​ k ​ W , k ​ W + U , 1 ) = ( θ ​ W , k ​ W + U ) , N = ( x ​ k ​ V , k ​ V + Z , 1 ) = ( θ ​ V , k ​ V + Z , 1 ) M=(xkW,kW+U,1)=(\theta W,kW+U),\quad N=(xkV,kV+Z,1)=(\theta V,kV+Z,1) where W = h ⁡ ( s + t ) s + h ​ t , U = s ​ t ​ ( 1 + h ) s + h ​ t , V = h ⁡ ( s + t ) t + h ​ s , Z = s ​ t ​ ( 1 + h ) t + h ​ s . W=\frac{h(s+t)}{s+ht},\quad U=\frac{st(1+h)}{s+ht},\quad V=\frac{h(s+t)}{t+hs},\quad Z=\frac{st(1+h)}{t+hs}. The conditions for a complete O’Nan configuration in unital 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} with ( a , b ) = ( 1 , b 0 + b 1 ​ δ ) (a,b)=(1,b_{0}+b_{1}\delta) are (using the notation as described in Section 2.1 ). ( θ ​ h ) 2 + b 1 ​ ( θ ​ h ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta h)^{2}}+b_{1}(\theta h)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ​ h \displaystyle\boxed{kh} (2) θ 2 + b 1 ​ θ q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{\theta^{2}}+b_{1}\theta^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k \displaystyle\boxed{k} (3) ( θ ​ W ) 2 + b 1 ​ ( θ ​ W ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta W)^{2}}+b_{1}(\theta W)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ​ W + U \displaystyle\boxed{kW}+\boxed{U} (4) ( θ ​ V ) 2 + b 1 ​ ( θ ​ V ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta V)^{2}}+b_{1}(\theta V)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ​ V + Z \displaystyle\boxed{kV}+\boxed{Z} (5) Note if we fix θ , h ∈ 𝔽 q 2 \theta,h\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} , then the first two equations determines k = k 0 + k 1 ​ δ k=k_{0}+k_{1}\delta , and k 0 k_{0} and k 1 k_{1} depend linearly on b 1 b_{1} . In this case, the LHS of ( 2 ) and ( 3 ) are fixed linear expressions in b 1 b_{1} and, as k + k q = k 1 k+k^{q}=k_{1} , ( 3 ) determines k 1 k_{1} and using ( 2 ), since k ​ h \boxed{kh} is the 𝔽 q \mathbb{F}_{q} -coefficient of δ \delta of the expression for k ​ h kh , i.e. k 0 ​ h 1 + k 1 ​ h 0 + k 1 ​ h 1 k_{0}h_{1}+k_{1}h_{0}+k_{1}h_{1} , this determines k 0 k_{0} and hence k k . Now suppose the above configuration, ( 1 , b , x , k , h , s , t ) (1,b,x,k,h,s,t) , is a complete BM-special Fano plane, and so the above equations hold. We will define a configuration in 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} with parameters ( 1 , b ′ , x ′ , k ′ , h , s , t ) (1,b^{\prime},x^{\prime},k^{\prime},h,s,t) with x ​ k = x ′ ​ k ′ xk=x^{\prime}k^{\prime} , and show that it is a complete BM-special Fano plane in 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} . The configuration 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} has the points X , Y , V X,Y,V in common with the configuration in 𝒰 a , b {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b} and P ′ = ( x ′ ​ k ′ ​ h , k ′ ​ h , 1 ) = ( θ ​ h , k ′ ​ h , 1 ) Q ′ = ( x ′ ​ k ′ , k ′ , 1 ) = ( θ , k ′ , 1 ) P^{\prime}=(x^{\prime}k^{\prime}h,k^{\prime}h,1)=(\theta h,k^{\prime}h,1)\quad Q^{\prime}=(x^{\prime}k^{\prime},k^{\prime},1)=(\theta,k^{\prime},1) and M ′ = ( θ ​ W , k ′ ​ W + U , 1 ) , N ′ = ( θ ​ V , k ′ ​ V + Z , 1 ) . M^{\prime}=(\theta W,k^{\prime}W+U,1),\quad N^{\prime}=(\theta V,k^{\prime}V+Z,1). (Note that W , U , V , Z W,U,V,Z only involve h , s , t h,s,t which are fixed for the two scenarios 𝒰 a , b , 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a,b},{\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} .) We require that the following equations hold for the configuration to be a Fano plane in 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} : ( θ ​ h ) 2 + b 1 ′ ​ ( θ ​ h ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta h)^{2}}+b_{1}^{\prime}(\theta h)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ′ ​ h \displaystyle\boxed{k^{\prime}h} (6) θ 2 + b 1 ′ ​ θ q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{\theta^{2}}+b_{1}^{\prime}\theta^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ′ \displaystyle\boxed{k^{\prime}} (7) ( θ ​ W ) 2 + b 1 ′ ​ ( θ ​ W ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta W)^{2}}+b_{1}^{\prime}(\theta W)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ′ ​ W + U \displaystyle\boxed{k^{\prime}W}+\boxed{U} (8) ( θ ​ V ) 2 + b 1 ′ ​ ( θ ​ V ) q + 1 \displaystyle\boxed{(\theta V)^{2}}+b_{1}^{\prime}(\theta V)^{q+1} = \displaystyle= k ′ ​ V + Z . \displaystyle\boxed{k^{\prime}V}+\boxed{Z}. (9) As before ( 6 ) and ( 7 ) determine k ′ k^{\prime} , and we define x ′ = θ / k ′ = x ​ k / k ′ x^{\prime}=\theta/k^{\prime}=xk/k^{\prime} , so that ( 6 ) and ( 7 ) hold and express that points P ′ , Q ′ P^{\prime},Q^{\prime} of the configuration lie in 𝒰 a ′ , b ′ {\mathcal{U}}_{a^{\prime},b^{\prime}} . Hence