y)=x^{\tau+1}+xy+y^{\tau} . As q = 2 2 ​ m + 1 q=2^{2m+1} is an odd power of 2, following Feng and Li [ 6 ] we suppose δ ∈ F 4 \delta\in{\mbox{F}}_{4} satisfies δ 2 = δ + 1 \delta^{2}=\delta+1 , further δ q = δ + 1 \delta^{q}=\delta+1 . Let τ : 𝔽 q 2 → 𝔽 q 2 , τ : x ↦ x 2 m + 1 , \tau\colon\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}}\rightarrow\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}},\quad\tau\colon x\mapsto x^{2^{m+1}}, so if x ∈ 𝔽 q x\in\mathbb{F}_{q} then x τ 2 = x 2 x^{\tau^{2}}=x^{2} . Here we give an example of a Fano plane contained in the Tits unital obtained by extending the Feng and Li construction. Example 1 . Suppose q = 8 q=8 , so q = 2 2 ​ m + 1 q=2^{2m+1} for m = 1 m=1 , and τ : x ↦ x 2 \tau\colon x\mapsto x^{2} for x ∈ 𝔽 q 2 x\in\mathbb{F}_{q^{2}} . Suppose w w is a generator of 𝔽 q ∗ \mathbb{F}_{q}^{*} , so w 7 = 1 w^{7}=1 and w 3 = w + 1 w^{3}=w+1 . Choose r 1 = w 2 r_{1}=w^{2} , r 1 = w 5 r_{1}=w^{5} , so we have V = ( 0 , 0 , 1 ) V=(0,0,1) R 1 = ( 0 , w 2 , 1 ) , R 2 = ( 0 , w 5 , 1 ) , P = ( 1 , e + w , 1 ) R_{1}=(0,w^{2},1),\ \ R_{2}=(0,w^{5},1),\ \ P=(1,e+w,1) and P 1 = ( w 5 + w 6 ​ δ , w 6 + δ , 1 ) , P 2 = ( w 3 + w 6 ​ δ , w 4 + δ , 1 ) , M = ( w 3 + w 5 ​ δ , 1 + δ , 1 ) P_{1}=(w^{5}+w^{6}\delta,w^{6}+\delta,1),\ \ P_{2}=(w^{3}+w^{6}\delta,w^{4}+\delta,1),\ \ M=(w^{3}+w^{5}\delta,1+\delta,1) We can check that P 1 ∈ 𝒰 T P_{1}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{T} , by calculating f ⁡ ( w 5 , w 6 ) = ( w 5 ) 4 + 2 + w 5 ​ w 6 + ( w 6 ) 4 = w 30 + w 11 + w 24 = w 2 + w 4 + w 3 = 1 f(w^{5},w^{6})=(w^{5})^{4+2}+w^{5}w^{6}+(w^{6})^{4}=w^{30}+w^{11}+w^{24}=w^{2}+w^{4}+w^{3}=1 using w 3 = w + 1 w^{3}=w+1 so w 4 = w 2 + w w^{4}=w^{2}+w . Looking at the δ \delta part of the second coordinate w 6 + δ w^{6}+\delta , this is 1, so this confirms that P 1 ∈ 𝒰 T P_{1}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{T} . Similarly for P 2 P_{2} . We now check M M f ⁡ ( w 3 , w 5 ) = w 18 + w 8 + w 20 = 1 f(w^{3},w^{5})=w^{18}+w^{8}+w^{20}=1 as required. This shows that the points all lie on U T U_{T} , and it is easy to check they form a Fano plane. Example 2 . Suppose now that q = 32 q=32 . We choose a generator w w of F 32 {\mbox{F}}_{32} satisfying w 5 = w 2 + 1 w^{5}=w^{2}+1 . Her we have r 1 = w 4 r_{1}=w^{4} and r 2 = w 5 r_{2}=w^{5} . We have τ : x ↦ x 2 3 = x 8 \tau\colon x\mapsto x^{2^{3}}=x^{8} . R 1 = ( 0 , w 4 , 1 ) , R 2 = ( 0 , w 5 , 1 ) , P = ( 1 , w 26 + e , 1 ) R_{1}=(0,w^{4},1),\ \ R_{2}=(0,w^{5},1),\ \ P=(1,w^{26}+e,1) and P 1 = ( w 11 + w 24 ​ δ , w 16 + w 29 ​ δ , 1 ) , P 2 = ( w 15 + w 8 ​ δ , w 20 + w 13 ​ δ , 1 ) , M = ( w 15 + δ , w 4 + w 29 ​ δ , 1 ) P_{1}=(w^{11}+w^{24}\delta,w^{16}+w^{29}\delta,1),\ \ P_{2}=(w^{15}+w^{8}\delta,w^{20}+w^{13}\delta,1),\ \ M=(w^{15}+\delta,w^{4}+w^{29}\delta,1) Computation shows that no O’Nan configuration contained in the Tits unital constructed by Feng and Li for the case q = 128 q=128 extends to a Fano plane. 5 Conclusion Firstly, consider the non-classical orthogonal BM unitals of even order. The O’Nans of Feng and Li have a line which contains the special point of the unital. We have shown their construction easily extends to a Fano plane contained in the unital, and consequently, contain an O’Nan configuration whose lines do not contain the special point. Further, we show that the number of these BM-special Fano planes in an orthogonal BM-unital depends only on the order of the unital, that is, it is the same for inequivalent orthogonal BM-unitals of the same even order. Note that by our calculations using Magma [ 7 ] we can find BM-ordinary Fano planes for small values of q q , however is is an open conjecture whether these exist for all even q q . Now consider the Tits unital. In this case computations have shown that the O’Nan configurations of Feng and Li complete to a Fano plane for q = 8 , 32 , 512 q=8,32,512 , and does not for q = 128 q=128 . Separate computations have shown however, that Fano planes exists in the Tits unital for q = 128 q=128 which necessarily is not one arising from the construction of Feng and Li. References [1] R.D Baker, G.L Ebert, On Buekenhout-Metz unitals of odd order, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A, Volume 60, Issue 1, 1992, Pages 67-84, ISSN 0097-3165, doi: https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(92)90038-V [2] S.G. Barwick, G.L. Ebert, Unitals in projective planes, in: Springer Monographs in Mathematics, Springer, New York, 2008 [3] L.D. Baumert, Cyclic Difference Sets (1971) https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:118191416 [4] F.!Buekenhout, Existence of unitals in finite translation planes of order q 2 q^{2} 2 with a kernel of order q q , Geom. Dedicata 5 (1976) 189–194. [5] G.L. Ebert, On Buekenhout-Metz unitals of even order, European Journal of Combinatorics (1992) 13 pp 109–117, doi: https://doi.org/10.1016/0195-6698(92)90042-X [6] Feng, Tao and Li, Weicong: On the Existence of O’Nan Configurations in Ovoidal Buekenhout–Metz Unitals in PG ​ ( 2 , q 2 ) {\textup{PG}}(2,q^{2}) Discrete Math. 342 (8) (2019) 2324–2332 doi:https://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2019.05.013 [7] Wieb Bosma, John Cannon, and Catherine Playoust, The Magma algebra system. I. The user language, J. Symbolic Comput., 24 (1997), 235–265. [8] R. Metz, On a class of unitals, Geom. Dedicata 8 (1979), 125–126 [9] M.E. O’Nan, Automorphisms of unitary block designs, J. Algebra 20 (1072) 495–511 [10] F. Piper, Unitary block designs, Graph Theorem Comb. 34 (1979) 98–105 Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the "Report Issue" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet.